{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgr4qBaxSEHpu3XfDFARk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GwbR3W2sCgbwXvGcrabwns","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"candidates":[{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.update","start_line":380,"end_line":410,"explanation":"On a password change, AuthService.update calls sessionService.deleteByUserIdWithExclude to end the user's other sessions. The exclusion is most likely wrong. It probably passes the wrong value as excludeId (for example the user id, or a missing or mismatched sessionId from the JWT payload) instead of userJwtPayload.sessionId. The current session is then deleted, and the next refresh returns 401. An older session whose id happens to equal the wrongly excluded value can survive."},{"file":"src/session/infrastructure/persistence/relational/repositories/session.repository.ts","symbol":"SessionRelationalRepository.deleteByUserIdWithExclude","start_line":85,"end_line":110,"explanation":"The delete filter may be wrong. For example, the Not(...) on the excluded id may be missing or inverted, or the user filter may be mismatched. That would delete the current session and keep an unrelated one."},{"file":"src/session/infrastructure/persistence/document/repositories/session.repository.ts","symbol":"SessionDocumentRepository.deleteByUserIdWithExclude","start_line":70,"end_line":94,"explanation":"This is the same exclusion logic for the Mongo backend. A faulty $ne/$not condition or an id type mismatch (ObjectId against string) would delete the current session and spare other ones."}],"fix":{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","description":"In AuthService.update, when the password changes, call sessionService.deleteByUserIdWithExclude({ userId: currentUser.id, excludeId: userJwtPayload.sessionId }). Make sure the repository filters on the user id and excludes only that session id, and that the id types match (Number for relatio